 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 505821,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Demonstrate  the density property of rational numbers by finding a rational number between <span data-math=\"\\frac{{27}}{{13}}\">\\frac{{27}}{{13}}</span> and <span data-math=\"\\frac{{21}}{{10}}\">\\frac{{21}}{{10}}</span>.",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>The density property process:</p>\n<p>Step 1: Add the two rational numbers.</p>\n<p><span data-math=\"\\frac{{27}}{{13}} + \\frac{{21}}{{10}} = \\frac{{27 \\times 10}}{{13 \\times 10}} + \\frac{{21 \\times 13}}{{10 \\times 13}}\"></span></p>\n<p><span data-math=\"\\frac{{270}}{{130}} + \\frac{{273}}{{130}}\"></span></p>\n<p><span data-math=\"\\frac{{543}}{{130}}\"></span></p>\n<p>Step 2: Divide that result by 2. Dividing by 2 is the same as multiplying by one-half.</p>\n<p><span data-math=\"\\frac{{543}}{{130}} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{{543}}{{260}}\"></span></p>\n<p>There are other numbers, but this is the one found using this process.</p>"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "The process used above yields <span data-math=\"\\frac{{543}}{{260}}\">\\frac{{543}}{{260}}</span>."
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  }
 ],
